# EigenScript gap: a string is a value with no object identity, so Lua's
# lua_topointer of a string (string.format("%p", s), manual 6.4.1: "a unique
# string identifier") cannot be the same for one long string and different
# for an equal copy. EigenLua hashes a short string's bytes (Lua interns
# those, so equal is identical) and gives each %p of a long string a fresh
# number, so even one long string formats differently twice. Not a #1286 row.
# Lua:  local s = string.rep("a", 100); local r = string.gsub(s, "b", "c")
#       print(string.format("%p", s) == string.format("%p", r)) -> true
#       (and string.format("%p", s) == string.format("%p", s) -> true)
# Expected: an identity (address or id) for a string value, or a builtin
# telling one string from an equal copy.
# Actual 686d3b7d: equal strings are indistinguishable, and the builtin index
# has no identity/address entry:
#   eigenscript --api | grep -ciE 'address|ptr|id_of|same_object'  ->  0
# Rows: pm.lua (413, 416, 426), literals.lua (225-228, unspecified), S.
a is "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa"
b is "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa" + "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa"
print of (a == b)
print of (type of a)
